      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "Label": "UNLABELLED",
          "content": "This study aimed to verify whether the distance walked is correlated with women's pain level throughout the active phase of labor."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODOLOGY",
          "content": "We realized an analytic, quasi-experimental intervention study. Study participants were 80 primiparous parturient women, who were admitted during spontaneous labor, with 37-42 weeks, at the start of the active phase."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DATA ANALYSIS",
          "content": "Spearman's correlation test."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "the parturient women walked an average distance of 1,624 meters, 63.09% of the active phase of labor and during an average time of five hours. Pain scores increase along with the advance in cervical dilatation. However, we only found a significant positive correlation when 5cm of dilatation had been reached, that is, the more distance the participants walked, the higher the pain scores they reached."
        }
      ]},
